---
# yamllint disable rule:line-length
# Widens the throwaway copy a guard makes of its item when the item turns out
# to be a `long` or a `double` (#1012).
#
# Three kinds of guard copy the item, hand the copy to a lambda and keep the
# original for whatever comes next: a filter (`281`/`283`/`413`), a peek, and a
# dropWhile (`310`). Each splices a plain `dup`, and each is right to, because
# when they fire the operation is still inside `211`/`214`'s boxing sandwich:
# the item they see is the WRAPPER, one slot wide. `411` is what changes the
# answer. It collapses the sandwich and retypes the whole run onto the raw
# primitive, and from that point the stack holds a two-slot value that a `dup`
# cannot copy: the verifier names the second half of that value on the stack
# and rejects it as "not assignable to category1 type".
#
# `281` and `413` already read a primitive rather than a wrapper and get this
# right on their own, which is why a stateless `LongStream.filter` has always
# fused. The copies that do not are the ones spliced while the wrapper was
# still the visible type, and rather than teach each splicing rule to predict
# `411`, the repair is made here, once, after `411` has decided.
#
# The anchor is the copy's CONSUMER, and only that: a `dup` immediately
# followed by an invokestatic taking ONE wide primitive and returning `Z` or
# `V` — which is exactly a filter or dropWhile predicate, or a peek body, being
# handed the copy. The copy IS that call's argument, so if the call takes a
# `long` the duplicated value is a `long`; nothing else needs to be known, and
# in particular not the distill's `bridge-input`. That matters: an unboxing
# `246` or `421` mints can leave a raw `long` on the stack inside a run whose
# `bridge-input` is still `Ljava/lang/Object;`, which is what a `distinct()`
# ahead of a `dropWhile` produces, and a rule keyed on `bridge-input` would
# walk past it.
#
# The one-parameter shape is deliberate. A CAPTURING guard's call takes the
# captured values first and the item last, so its first parameter says nothing
# about what was copied; `314` builds those and parks the item in a local
# instead. Requiring `(J)Z`, `(J)V`, `(D)Z` or `(D)V` keeps this rule to the
# non-capturing guards, and being closed on `dup` keeps it from firing twice on
# its own output.
#
# Only the copy moves. The wrapper's own stack arrangement (`512`'s `dup_x2`
# for a wide consumer) is already width-correct, and a guard's other opcodes
# work on the latch array and the verdict, both narrow whatever the item is.
name: widen-guard-dup
pattern: |
  ⟦
    φ ↦ Φ.hone.distill,
    class ↦ 𝑒-class,
    bridge-input ↦ 𝑒-bridge-input,
    bridge-output ↦ 𝑒-bridge-output,
    start ↦ 𝑒-start,
    accepted ↦ 𝑒-accepted,
    returned ↦ 𝑒-returned,
    static ↦ 𝑒-static,
    state ↦ 𝑒-state,
    body ↦ ⟦
      𝐵-before,
      𝜏-dup ↦ ⟦ φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.dup ⟧,
      𝜏-call ↦ ⟦
        φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.invokestatic,
        i2 ↦ 𝑒-call-class,
        i3 ↦ 𝑒-call-method,
        i4 ↦ 𝑒-call-signature,
        i5 ↦ 𝑒-call-is-interface
      ⟧,
      𝐵-after
    ⟧
  ⟧
result: |
  ⟦
    φ ↦ Φ.hone.distill,
    class ↦ 𝑒-class,
    bridge-input ↦ 𝑒-bridge-input,
    bridge-output ↦ 𝑒-bridge-output,
    start ↦ 𝑒-start,
    accepted ↦ 𝑒-accepted,
    returned ↦ 𝑒-returned,
    static ↦ 𝑒-static,
    state ↦ 𝑒-state,
    body ↦ ⟦
      𝐵-before,
      𝜏-dup ↦ ⟦ φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.dup2 ⟧,
      𝜏-call ↦ ⟦
        φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.invokestatic,
        i2 ↦ 𝑒-call-class,
        i3 ↦ 𝑒-call-method,
        i4 ↦ 𝑒-call-signature,
        i5 ↦ 𝑒-call-is-interface
      ⟧,
      𝐵-after
    ⟧
  ⟧
when:
  matches: ['^\((J|D)\)[ZV]$', 𝑒-call-signature]
